  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "An annual flu shot is recommended for everyone 6 months and older, ideally by the end of October, to protect against, seasonal,  influenza viruses. Vaccines are updated yearly, with the 2025-26 season featuring trivalent vaccines to guard against three main virus strains. They are crucial for reducing severe illness and hospitalization, especially for high-risk groups. Key Details About Flu Shots Effectiveness: It takes about two weeks for antibodies to develop after vaccination. Availability: Shots are available at pharmacies, doctor's offices, and health departments. Cost: Most insurance plans, including Medicare Part B, cover the vaccine. Types: Options include standard inactivated shots (needle) and rarely, nasal sprays for, specific, age groups. High-Risk Groups: Vaccination is particularly important for adults over 65, children under 5, pregnant people, and those with chronic health conditions. Common Misconceptions & Tips No Flu: The vaccine cannot cause the flu. Side Effects: Side effects are usually mild and short-lived. Timing: While October is ideal, vaccination should continue throughout the season as long as viruses are circulating. Simultaneous Vaccination: You can receive a COVID-19 vaccine and a flu shot at the same time.",
